###  urn:gs1:std:gdsn:cv168407
gdsn:cv168407 rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                       gdsn:c-2060056022 ;
              dct:source "gdsn_codeValues.json" ;
              rdfs:comment "A thumb tack (North American English) or push pin is a short nail or pin used to fasten items to a wall or board for display and intended to be inserted by hand, generally without the assistance of tools. A variety of names are used to refer to different designs intended for various purpose. Thumb tack and push pin are both sometimes compounded (thumbtack or pushpin) or hyphenated (thumb-tack or push-pin). Thumb tacks made of brass, tin or iron may be referred to as brass tacks, brass pins, tin tacks or iron tacks, respectively. Drawing pin or drawing-pin refers to thumb tacks used to hold drawings on drawing boards. Map pin or map tack refers to thumb tacks used to mark locations on a map and to hold the map in place." ;
              rdfs:label "PIN_PUSH" ;
              gdsn:codeListName "ClosureOrFastenerTypeCode" ;
              gdsn:sourceId "168407" ;
              gdsn:sourceType "codeValue" .


###  urn:gs1:std:gdsn:cv168408
gdsn:cv168408 rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                       gdsn:c-2060056022 ;
              dct:source "gdsn_codeValues.json" ;
              rdfs:comment "The safety pin, also known as a baby pin, is a variation of the regular pin which includes a simple spring mechanism and a clasp. The clasp serves two purposes: to form a closed loop thereby properly fastening the pin to whatever it is applied to, and to cover the end of the pin to protect the user from the sharp point. Safety pins are commonly used to fasten pieces of fabric or clothing together. Safety pins, or more usually a special version with an extra safe cover, called a nappy pin were widely used to fasten cloth diapers (nappies), as the safety clasp prevents the baby from being jabbed. Similarly, they can be used to patch torn or damaged clothing. Safety pins can also be used as an accessory or jewelry, such as earrings, chains, and wristbands. Sometimes they are used to attach an embroidered patch." ;
              rdfs:label "PIN_SAFETY" ;
              gdsn:codeListName "ClosureOrFastenerTypeCode" ;
              gdsn:sourceId "168408" ;
              gdsn:sourceType "codeValue" .


###  urn:gs1:std:gdsn:cv168409
gdsn:cv168409 rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                       gdsn:c-2060056022 ;
              dct:source "gdsn_codeValues.json" ;
              rdfs:comment "A pin is a device used for fastening objects or material together. Pins often have two components: a body or shaft with a sharp tip and a head or crown at the opposite end. The sharpened body penetrates the material, while the head provides a driving surface as wel las preventing the pin from oushing all the way through the material." ;
              rdfs:label "PIN_STRAIGHT" ;
              gdsn:codeListName "ClosureOrFastenerTypeCode" ;
              gdsn:sourceId "168409" ;
              gdsn:sourceType "codeValue" .


###  urn:gs1:std:gdsn:cv168410
gdsn:cv168410 rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                       gdsn:c-2060056022 ;
              dct:source "gdsn_codeValues.json" ;
              rdfs:comment "A rubber band, also known as a binder, elastic band, lackey band, laggy band, or elastic, is a short length of rubber and latex, elastic in nature and formed in the shape of a circle which is commonly used to hold multiple objects together." ;
              rdfs:label "RUBBER_BAND" ;
              gdsn:codeListName "ClosureOrFastenerTypeCode" ;
              gdsn:sourceId "168410" ;
              gdsn:sourceType "codeValue" .


###  urn:gs1:std:gdsn:cv168411
gdsn:cv168411 rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                       gdsn:c-2060056022 ;
              dct:source "gdsn_codeValues.json" ;
              rdfs:comment "Snaps are fasteners that should be used to secure areas where there is little strain. They are primarily used to hold something in place, and can therefore be used in place of buttons and buttonholes or selfgripping devices. The sew-in styles are generally in two parts: the ball and the socket (Figure 6). The ball part of the snap is positioned on the overlap side of the placket, and the socket on the underlap." ;
              rdfs:label "SNAP" ;
              gdsn:codeListName "ClosureOrFastenerTypeCode" ;
              gdsn:sourceId "168411" ;
              gdsn:sourceType "codeValue" .


###  urn:gs1:std:gdsn:cv168412
gdsn:cv168412 rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                       gdsn:c-2060056022 ;
              dct:source "gdsn_codeValues.json" ;
              rdfs:comment "This type of product is manufactured in 3 standards sizes of open prongs, LIGNE 14, LIGNE15 and LIGNE 16. The snap is produced on this product by the construction of a petal like socket (female) which engages the stud (male). This product is suitable for use on knitted fabrics due to the construction of the five pronged ring which attaches with either the socket or the stud. A LIGNE is a measurement equivalent to 1/40th of an inch and the size of the product relates to the diameter of the open prong." ;
              rdfs:label "SNAP_PRONG_TYPE_PRESS_STUD" ;
              gdsn:codeListName "ClosureOrFastenerTypeCode" ;
              gdsn:sourceId "168412" ;
              gdsn:sourceType "codeValue" .


###  urn:gs1:std:gdsn:cv168413
gdsn:cv168413 rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                       gdsn:c-2060056022 ;
              dct:source "gdsn_codeValues.json" ;
              rdfs:comment "A two part snap fastener so called as the socket (female part) incorporates a spring which engages the stud (male part) to securely fasten the item." ;
              rdfs:label "SNAP_SPRING_TYPE" ;
              gdsn:codeListName "ClosureOrFastenerTypeCode" ;
              gdsn:sourceId "168413" ;
              gdsn:sourceType "codeValue" .


###  urn:gs1:std:gdsn:cv168414
gdsn:cv168414 rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                       gdsn:c-2060056022 ;
              dct:source "gdsn_codeValues.json" ;
              rdfs:comment "A staple is a type of two-pronged fastener, usually metal, used for joining or binding materials together. Large staples might be used with a hammer or staple gun for masonry, roofing, corrugated boxes and other heavy-duty uses. Smaller staples are used with a stapler to attach pieces of paper together; such staples are a permanent and durable fastener for paper documents, unlike the paper clip." ;
              rdfs:label "STAPLE" ;
              gdsn:codeListName "ClosureOrFastenerTypeCode" ;
              gdsn:sourceId "168414" ;
              gdsn:sourceType "codeValue" .


###  urn:gs1:std:gdsn:cv168415
gdsn:cv168415 rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                       gdsn:c-2060056022 ;
              dct:source "gdsn_codeValues.json" ;
              rdfs:comment "A strap, sometimes also called strop, is an elongated flap or ribbon of material. Thin straps are used as part of clothing or baggage, or bedding such as a sleeping bag (For example spaghetti strap, shoulder strap). A strap differs from a belt mainly in that a strap is usually integral to the item. Straps are also used as fasteners to attach and bind items, to objects, animals (for example a saddle on a horse) and people (for example a watch on a wrist). Occasionally a strap is specified after what it binds or holds, e.g. chin strap." ;
              rdfs:label "STRAP" ;
              gdsn:codeListName "ClosureOrFastenerTypeCode" ;
              gdsn:sourceId "168415" ;
              gdsn:sourceType "codeValue" .


###  urn:gs1:std:gdsn:cv168416
gdsn:cv168416 rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                       gdsn:c-2060056022 ;
              dct:source "gdsn_codeValues.json" ;
              rdfs:comment "Whether it is for decoration or as a substitute for a metal eye, a thread eye can be used on a garment. The thread eyes are not as strong as a metal eye, but can be less conspicuous, and are therefore recommended for use in areas that do not have a lot of pull or strain, such as a neckline opening above a zipper." ;
              rdfs:label "THREAD_EYES" ;
              gdsn:codeListName "ClosureOrFastenerTypeCode" ;
              gdsn:sourceId "168416" ;
              gdsn:sourceType "codeValue" .


###  urn:gs1:std:gdsn:cv168417
gdsn:cv168417 rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                       gdsn:c-2060056022 ;
              dct:source "gdsn_codeValues.json" ;
              rdfs:comment "A piece of material which can be tied around an opening or is integrated as a handle which can be tied at an opening to close the item." ;
              rdfs:label "TIE_OR_TIE_HANDLE" ;
              gdsn:codeListName "ClosureOrFastenerTypeCode" ;
              gdsn:sourceId "168417" ;
              gdsn:sourceType "codeValue" .


###  urn:gs1:std:gdsn:cv168418
gdsn:cv168418 rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                       gdsn:c-2060056022 ;
              dct:source "gdsn_codeValues.json" ;
              rdfs:comment "In contrast to the disc-shaped button, the toggle is usually long and narrow. The toggle fastenings insert through loops of rope or leather to help fasten an item." ;
              rdfs:label "TOGGLE_FASTENERS" ;
              gdsn:codeListName "ClosureOrFastenerTypeCode" ;
              gdsn:sourceId "168418" ;
              gdsn:sourceType "codeValue" .


###  urn:gs1:std:gdsn:cv168419
gdsn:cv168419 rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                       gdsn:c-2060056022 ;
              dct:source "gdsn_codeValues.json" ;
              rdfs:comment "A treasury tag or India tag is an item of stationery used to fasten sheets of paper together or to a folder. A treasury tag is a lace with a sharp metal tag at one end which could be threaded through the holes in a stack of documents or cards and then inserted into a female tag at the other end to form a loop, so binding the documents. The tags in that case were in line with the string, like a shoelace. An India tag was similar but the metal tags were orthogonal to the string, so forming a cross-piece. The India tag did not form a loop as the cross-pieces were sufficiently wide that they did not slip back through the holes" ;
              rdfs:label "TREASURY_TAG" ;
              gdsn:codeListName "ClosureOrFastenerTypeCode" ;
              gdsn:sourceId "168419" ;
              gdsn:sourceType "codeValue" .


###  urn:gs1:std:gdsn:cv168420
gdsn:cv168420 rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                       gdsn:c-2060056022 ;
              dct:source "gdsn_codeValues.json" ;
              rdfs:comment "A zipper, zip, fly or zip fastener, formerly known as a clasp locker, is a commonly used device for binding the edges of an opening of fabric or other flexible material, as on a garment or a bag. A zipper is based on interlocking teeth. Initially it was called the “hookless fastener” and was later redesigned to become more reliable." ;
              rdfs:label "ZIPPER" ;
              gdsn:codeListName "ClosureOrFastenerTypeCode" ;
              gdsn:sourceId "168420" ;
              gdsn:sourceType "codeValue" .


###  urn:gs1:std:gdsn:cv168421
gdsn:cv168421 rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                       gdsn:c2147348215 ;
              dct:source "gdsn_codeValues.json" ;
              rdfs:comment "Jacket or Coat with a hood, often lined with fur or faux fur." ;
              rdfs:label "ANORAK" ;
