###  urn:gs1:std:gdsn:cv169827
gdsn:cv169827 rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                       gdsn:c1041249980 ;
              dct:source "gdsn_codeValues.json" ;
              rdfs:comment "Belgian Style Blonde ales are very pale in colour. They tend to be clear, crisp, and dry, with low to medium bitterness and aroma from hops, and some sweetness from malt." ;
              rdfs:label "BELGIAN_STYLE_BLONDE_ALE" ;
              gdsn:codeListName "BeerStyleTypeCode" ;
              gdsn:sourceId "169827" ;
              gdsn:sourceType "codeValue" .


###  urn:gs1:std:gdsn:cv169828
gdsn:cv169828 rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                       gdsn:c1041249980 ;
              dct:source "gdsn_codeValues.json" ;
              rdfs:comment "Belgian Style Flanders Oud Bruin or Oud Red ale, is a style of sour ale, crafted originally in Belgium. Flanders red ale is fermented with organisms other than Saccharomyces cerevisiae, especially Lactobacillus, which produces a sour character attributable to lactic acid." ;
              rdfs:label "BELGIAN_STYLE_FLANDERS_OUD_BRUIN_OR_OUD_RED_ALE" ;
              gdsn:codeListName "BeerStyleTypeCode" ;
              gdsn:sourceId "169828" ;
              gdsn:sourceType "codeValue" .


###  urn:gs1:std:gdsn:cv169829
gdsn:cv169829 rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                       gdsn:c1041249980 ;
              dct:source "gdsn_codeValues.json" ;
              rdfs:comment "Most fruit beers are ales however, they typically do not carry an ale character. In order to allow for the fruit flavour to come through nicely, the malt’s flavour is not dominant and there is a low bitterness level to the beer." ;
              rdfs:label "BELGIAN_STYLE_FRUIT_BEER" ;
              gdsn:codeListName "BeerStyleTypeCode" ;
              gdsn:sourceId "169829" ;
              gdsn:sourceType "codeValue" .


###  urn:gs1:std:gdsn:cv169830
gdsn:cv169830 rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                       gdsn:c1041249980 ;
              dct:source "gdsn_codeValues.json" ;
              rdfs:comment "Belgian Style Fruit Lambic beers, originally crafted in Belgium, include gueuze and kriek Lambic and infused with fruit flavours. Lambic differs from most other beers in that it is fermented through exposure to wild yeasts and bacteria, as opposed to exposure to carefully cultivated strains of brewer's yeast. This process gives the beer its distinctive flavour: dry, vinous, and cidery, usually with a sour aftertaste." ;
              rdfs:label "BELGIAN_STYLE_FRUIT_LAMBIC" ;
              gdsn:codeListName "BeerStyleTypeCode" ;
              gdsn:sourceId "169830" ;
              gdsn:sourceType "codeValue" .


###  urn:gs1:std:gdsn:cv169831
gdsn:cv169831 rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                       gdsn:c1041249980 ;
              dct:source "gdsn_codeValues.json" ;
              rdfs:comment "Belgian Style Gueuze is a type of Lambic beer, originally crafted in Belgium. It is made by blending young (1 year old) and old (2 to 3 year old) Lambic, which is bottled for a second fermentation. Because the young Lambic are not fully fermented, the blended beer contains fermentable sugars, which allow a second fermentation to occur." ;
              rdfs:label "BELGIAN_STYLE_GUEUZE_LAMBIC" ;
              gdsn:codeListName "BeerStyleTypeCode" ;
              gdsn:sourceId "169831" ;
              gdsn:sourceType "codeValue" .


###  urn:gs1:std:gdsn:cv169832
gdsn:cv169832 rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                       gdsn:c1041249980 ;
              dct:source "gdsn_codeValues.json" ;
              rdfs:comment "Belgian Style Lambic beers, originally crafted in Belgium, include gueuze and kriek Lambic. Lambic differs from most other beers in that it is fermented through exposure to wild yeasts and bacteria, as opposed to exposure to carefully cultivated strains of brewer's yeast. This process gives the beer its distinctive flavour: dry, vinous, and cidery, usually with a sour aftertaste." ;
              rdfs:label "BELGIAN_STYLE_LAMBIC" ;
              gdsn:codeListName "BeerStyleTypeCode" ;
              gdsn:sourceId "169832" ;
              gdsn:sourceType "codeValue" .


###  urn:gs1:std:gdsn:cv169833
gdsn:cv169833 rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                       gdsn:c1041249980 ;
              dct:source "gdsn_codeValues.json" ;
              rdfs:comment "Belgian Style Pale ale is a top fermented beer, crafted originally in Belgium, made with predominantly pale malt. The highest proportion of pale malts results in a lighter colour." ;
              rdfs:label "BELGIAN_STYLE_PALE_ALE" ;
              gdsn:codeListName "BeerStyleTypeCode" ;
              gdsn:sourceId "169833" ;
              gdsn:sourceType "codeValue" .


###  urn:gs1:std:gdsn:cv169834
gdsn:cv169834 rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                       gdsn:c1041249980 ;
              dct:source "gdsn_codeValues.json" ;
              rdfs:comment "Belgian Style Witbier, white beer, bière blanche, or simply witte is a barley/wheat, top fermented beer, crafted originally in Belgium. It gets its name from the suspended yeast and wheat proteins which cause the beer to look hazy, or white, when cold. It is a descendant from those medieval beers which were flavoured and preserved with a blend of spices and other plants such as coriander, orange, and bitter orange referred to as \"gruit\" instead of using hops." ;
              rdfs:label "BELGIAN_STYLE_WITBIER" ;
              gdsn:codeListName "BeerStyleTypeCode" ;
              gdsn:sourceId "169834" ;
              gdsn:sourceType "codeValue" .


###  urn:gs1:std:gdsn:cv169835
gdsn:cv169835 rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                       gdsn:c1041249980 ;
              dct:source "gdsn_codeValues.json" ;
              rdfs:comment "Weisse is a cloudy, sour beer of around 3% alcohol by volume." ;
              rdfs:label "BERLINER_STYLE_WEISSE" ;
              gdsn:codeListName "BeerStyleTypeCode" ;
              gdsn:sourceId "169835" ;
              gdsn:sourceType "codeValue" .


###  urn:gs1:std:gdsn:cv169836
gdsn:cv169836 rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                       gdsn:c1041249980 ;
              dct:source "gdsn_codeValues.json" ;
              rdfs:comment "Bitter is a British style of pale ale that varies in colour from gold to dark amber, and in strength typically from 3% to 5.5% alcohol by volume." ;
              rdfs:label "BITTER" ;
              gdsn:codeListName "BeerStyleTypeCode" ;
              gdsn:sourceId "169836" ;
              gdsn:sourceType "codeValue" .


###  urn:gs1:std:gdsn:cv169837
gdsn:cv169837 rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                       gdsn:c1041249980 ;
              dct:source "gdsn_codeValues.json" ;
              rdfs:comment "Bohemian Style Pilseners (Pilsner) are made with neutral and hard water. They tend to be golden in colour with a dry, crisp, and somewhat bitter flavour. Pilsener stands out from other lagers due to its more distinctive hop taste." ;
              rdfs:label "BOHEMIAN_STYLE_PILSENER" ;
              gdsn:codeListName "BeerStyleTypeCode" ;
              gdsn:sourceId "169837" ;
              gdsn:sourceType "codeValue" .


###  urn:gs1:std:gdsn:cv169838
gdsn:cv169838 rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                       gdsn:c1041249980 ;
              dct:source "gdsn_codeValues.json" ;
              rdfs:comment "Brett beers vary in colour and can take on the hues of added fruits or other ingredients. Horsey, goaty, leathery, phenolic and some fruity acidic character derived from Brettanomyces organisms may be evident, but in balance with other components of a Brett beer." ;
              rdfs:label "BRETT_BEER" ;
              gdsn:codeListName "BeerStyleTypeCode" ;
              gdsn:sourceId "169838" ;
              gdsn:sourceType "codeValue" .


